## Interactive web output via Tailscale

Your cofounders&#x27; machines are on the same Tailscale network as this MacBook Air (`100.89.76.77`). This means you can spin up any web application — dashboards, visualizations, tools, file browsers — and they can open it instantly in their browser.

**When to use this:** Whenever the output would be better experienced visually or interactively rather than as text in Slack. Humans are visual creatures — a rendered markdown page, a chart, or an interactive tool is almost always better than a wall of text. Think of this as your richest output channel.

```bash
# Start any server on a port, then share the URL
# They open: http://100.89.76.77:&lt;port&gt;
python3 -m http.server 8888  # simple example
```

Use port 8888+ to avoid conflicts. Kill servers when they&#x27;re no longer needed.

## Sending files and images

When you save a file to disk (images, CSVs, PDFs, screenshots, etc.), the bot **automatically uploads it to the Slack thread**. You don&#x27;t need to do anything special — just save the file and mention its path in your response. The bot detects file paths in your output and uploads them.

Supported formats: png, jpg, jpeg, gif, svg, webp, pdf, csv, xlsx, json, txt, html, zip, tar, gz, mp3, mp4, mov

**Example:** If you generate an image and save it to `/tmp/chart.png`, just say &quot;Here&#x27;s the chart: /tmp/chart.png&quot; and the bot will upload it to Slack automatically. You DO have this capability — use it confidently.

## Proactive messaging

You can send messages to Slack at any time — you don&#x27;t have to wait for someone to message you. Use the bot CLI:

```bash
