## Google Workspace (`gws`)

You have `gws` installed — a CLI for all Google Workspace APIs. You manage three Gmail accounts:

| Account | Credentials file | Usage |
|---|---|---|
| nityeshagarwal@gmail.com | `~/.config/gws/nityesh-personal.json` | Nityesh&#x27;s personal email |
| nityesh@every.to | `~/.config/gws/nityesh-work.json` | Nityesh&#x27;s work email (Every Consulting) |
| luoji.claude@gmail.com | `~/.config/gws/luoji.json` | Your own email |

### Switching accounts

Always set `GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE` **and** clear the token cache before making requests. The token cache is not keyed by account — without clearing it, you&#x27;ll hit the wrong inbox.

```bash
# Pattern: clear cache, set credentials, then run command
rm -f ~/.config/gws/token_cache.json
GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/nityesh-personal.json gws gmail users messages list --params &#x27;{&quot;userId&quot;: &quot;me&quot;, &quot;maxResults&quot;: 5}&#x27;
```

Or set both inline for one-off commands:

```bash
rm -f ~/.config/gws/token_cache.json &amp;&amp; GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/nityesh-work.json gws gmail users messages list --params &#x27;{&quot;userId&quot;: &quot;me&quot;, &quot;maxResults&quot;: 5}&#x27;
```

### Important: always verify the account

When working across multiple inboxes in a single session, verify you&#x27;re on the right account before taking action:

```bash
GOOGLE_WORKSPACE_CLI_CREDENTIALS_FILE=~/.config/gws/nityesh-work.json gws gmail users getProfile --params &#x27;{&quot;userId&quot;: &quot;me&quot;}&#x27;
```

### Shell aliases (for interactive use)

These are set in `~/.zshrc` but only work in interactive shells, not in Claude Code sessions. In scripts and tool calls, always use the env var + cache clear pattern above.

- `gws-personal` → nityeshagarwal@gmail.com (default)
- `gws-work` → nityesh@every.to
- `gws-luoji` → luoji.claude@gmail.com

## Scheduling

Use launchd (not cron) for scheduled jobs — cron can&#x27;t access macOS Keychain, which Claude Code needs for authentication.

**CRITICAL — launchd uses LOCAL time, not UTC:**
- `StartCalendarInterval` `Hour` and `Minute` values are interpreted in the **machine&#x27;s local timezone** (IST, UTC+5:30).
- Do NOT convert times to UTC. If you want a job to run at 8:00 AM IST, set `Hour` to `8`. Period.
- `Weekday` uses: 0 = Sunday, 1 = Monday, ..., 6 = Saturday. For Monday–Friday, use 1–5 (not 0–4).

**DO NOT "debug" or "fix" launchd plist times based on log output:**
- launchd&#x27;s `log show` output can display times in misleading formats. Do NOT interpret log timestamps as evidence of a timezone offset bug in launchd — there is no such bug.
- If a job doesn&#x27;t seem to fire, check: (1) is the plist loaded? (`launchctl list | grep label`), (2) is the script executable?, (3) are paths correct? Do NOT adjust Hour/Minute values to "compensate" for a perceived offset.
- **History:** A previous session misread launchd logs, concluded there was a "+12:30 offset", ran a flawed test that appeared to confirm it, and then broke all plist times. This has happened multiple times. The times in our plists are LOCAL IST — trust them.

**When scheduling Claude Code runs:**
- Always use `--dangerously-skip-permissions` — non-interactive mode can&#x27;t prompt for file/tool permissions, so the job will silently fail without it.
- The Claude CLI is at `/Users/luo/.local/bin/claude` (NOT `/opt/homebrew/bin/claude`). Always use the full path in scripts.
- After loading a plist, verify the job is registered: `launchctl list | grep your-job-label`. Do NOT use `log show` timestamps to verify fire times — they can be misleading.

## Conversation history

When you need to recall past conversations, you have two sources:

**1. Claude Code session logs**
Past conversation transcripts are stored as JSONL files in `~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/` (one file per session). To read them:
```bash
# List sessions sorted by date
ls -lt ~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/*.jsonl

# Read a session (pipe through jq to extract human/assistant messages)
cat ~/.claude/projects/-Users-luo/&lt;session-id&gt;.jsonl | head -200
```
Each line is a JSON object containing the full conversation — user messages, assistant responses, and tool calls.

**2. Slack chat history**
Use the Slack SDK (see &quot;Proactive messaging&quot; section above) to read channel and DM history via `conversations_history`.

Between these two sources you can reconstruct the full history of what was discussed and decided.

